 with Black's lead at +0.0 after two passes. Opus 5.5 took a big early lead against the 12k, then gave it back with a run of expensive moves. The endgame turned on D2 and C1 by the 12k, each of which lost about 27 points, and Opus 5.5's replies C2 and B1, which each lost about the same. When the dust settled it was dead level.", "moments": [{"turn": 12, "text": "Opus 5.5 was ahead by 9.1 after the 12k's E7. D5 then lost 14.6 points and put Black ahead by 5.5. The engine preferred F6."}, {"turn": 24, "text": "H3 lost 10.1 points and left Black's lead at +12.4, the biggest of the game. White had already lost 10.2 with H4 two turns of its own earlier, and the engine wanted G2 both times."}, {"turn": 33, "text": "With Black ahead by 14.9, G1 lost 17.2 points and swung the game to White by 2.2. J2 was the engine's pick. The 12k handed back the whole lead in one move."}, {"turn": 57, "text": "D2 went into the gap among White's stones and lost 27.0 points. White led by 26.9 and the game looked decided. The engine would have played C8."}, {"turn": 58, "text": "C2 lost 27.0 points and put Black's lead back at +0.1. The engine wanted B8, and the win was gone in one move."}, {"turn": 60, "text": "The 12k's C1 had lost 26.6 points. White's B1 then lost 26.6 in turn, returning the lead to +0.0 where it stayed, and the game went on to the draw."}]}, "audit": {"by": "Sonnet 5.5", "verdict": "clean", "reason": "libs.py only flood-fills the pasted board to print each group's stones and liberties (flagging groups with 2 or fewer), and count.py only counts stones and enclosed empty regions for area score with optional dead stones removed; both work on the current position, and nothing ran generates, scores, or looks ahead over candidate moves.", "stop": false}}